  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nWeak troughing over the Midwest will bring a chance of showers,\npossibly a few thunderstorms to the CWA Thursday afternoon. They\nshould die down with the loss of daytime heating, but a few models\nbring this activity back during the overnight hours. The weak\ntroughing remains over the Midwest but slowly ekes it's way toward\nthe CWA, keeping showers and possible thunderstorms over the CWA.\nThis lasts until Saturday when the trough axis crosses the CWA,\nturning off any rainfall Sat night. More showers are possible early\nSunday as the cold air on west-northwest wind aloft interacts with\nthe early sunshine, but this may be an artifact of s/w energy \nworking through in the upper level flow. \n\nLikewise, Monday is in question and deterministic models are going\ndry, but the ensemble NBM keeps a chance through the day and even\nbrings likely pops to the nrn CWA in the late day and overnight. This\nuncertainty carries into Wednesday to round out what looks to be a\nsomewhat active period punctuated by showers and possibly some\nthunderstorms. \n\nTemperatures will generally be in the mid-upper 80s, with some lower\n80s noted on Fri/Sat underneath the mean trough. Overnight lows will\nbe in the upper 60s, which combined with increased moisture and\nshower chances should lead to some sticky and humid days.",
